Recorded output, and what was NOT recorded.

Every file here says at its top where it came from. Two of them are CONSTRUCTED
rather than recorded, and they say so in capitals, because this repository's
whole discipline is that a claim carries its measurement — and a fixture that
looks recorded and is not is a measurement that never happened.

  RECORDED, on 2026-08-27, in a container on an Apple Silicon Mac
  (Docker Desktop, aarch64, ubuntu:26.04, kernel 7.0.12-linuxkit):

    sysfs-recorded.txt              /sys/bus/{pci,usb}/devices, every attribute
                                    this module reads, and the driver symlink
                                    targets verbatim
    dkms-status-mixed.txt           dkms 3.2.2, three modules deliberately put
                                    into three different states
    dkms-status-added-only.txt      the same machine asked about a kernel it has
                                    no builds for -- the trap
    ubuntu-drivers-refusal.txt      ubuntu-drivers declining to answer, in prose,
                                    on stdout
    pci.ids-excerpt                 lines copied verbatim out of pci.ids
                                    2026.02.12 as shipped by the pci.ids package

  CONSTRUCTED, and here is exactly how far the construction can be trusted:

    sysfs-constructed.txt           An NVIDIA card, a Bluetooth dongle with no
                                    driver, a card whose driver is a DKMS
                                    module. No such hardware was available to
                                    this session. The ATTRIBUTE NAMES and the
                                    SHAPES are the recorded ones above; the ids
                                    are real ids from pci.ids; the states are
                                    the four this feature exists for. What it
                                    does NOT prove is that a real RTX 3060
                                    presents itself this way.
    ubuntu-drivers-devices.txt      Written against ubuntu-drivers-common
                                    1:0.10.9's OWN SOURCE -- the print
                                    statements in command_devices() were read
                                    line by line and the format string
                                    "%-9s: %s -%s" and the flag order
                                    (distro/third-party, free/non-free, builtin,
                                    recommended) reproduced from them. That is
                                    stronger than guessing and weaker than a
                                    recording, and the difference matters if
                                    ubuntu-drivers ever changes the code.

The machine this was recorded on has no discrete GPU, no DKMS-backed device and
no unsupported hardware, which is why the interesting half had to be
constructed. installer/testing/check-device-logic.py is where the STATE RULES
are checked; these fixtures are where the PARSERS are.
